{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_09b2f4ca228b",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 223,
      "mechanism": "The source interface state machine (lines 180-252) lacks reset initialization for drop_reg and select_reg. During early cycles under reset, these registers power up in an unknown state (X in simulation), causing the combinational select_valid_next logic to compute incorrect grant/valid decisions. This corrupts the int_axis_tvalid matrix, starving the arbiter and leaving m_axis_tvalid low at cycles 4-5. Adding explicit reset of drop_reg/select_reg to 0 clears the stale selection state so the first valid transaction is routed correctly."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_b00008c7be19",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 301,
      "mechanism": "The arbiter index encoding uses S_COUNT (value 4) for mask shift width and rotation, but the grant_encoded index only spans CL_S_COUNT=2 bits. This overshoot produces a mask of {4{1'b1}} >> (4-idx) instead of the correct {4{1'b1}} >> (2-idx) wrap, breaking round-robin fairness and causing the grant to stall on the same source. Replacing the shift width with CL_S_COUNT (and the companion source-side index width with CL_M_COUNT) restores proper round-robin pointer update so m_axis_tvalid asserts on schedule."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_0cffe2701cd6",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 210,
      "mechanism": "The destination-range check (lines 208-215) is evaluated combinationally even during reset, allowing a spurious select_valid_next pulse when rst is high. This early pulse propagates through int_axis_tvalid into the arbiter, creating a phantom grant that desynchronizes the pipeline and delays the real m_axis_tvalid by one cycle. Gating the entire match block with && !rst prevents reset-cycle noise from entering the routing logic."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_0954daa92ecd",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 233,
      "mechanism": "The source-side valid-to-ready assignment int_axis_tvalid[m*M_COUNT +: M_COUNT] uses M_COUNT=1 as the part-select stride, yet the vector is logically organized as S_COUNT rows of M_COUNT columns (row-major). The correct row stride is S_COUNT; using M_COUNT collapses all source rows onto the same bit when S_COUNT>1, losing valid signals and starving the output. Changing the slice step to S_COUNT restores the intended matrix layout."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_af2778ae10dd",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 233,
      "mechanism": "Coordinate fix for both matrix strides: the source-side valid vector is sliced with CL_S_COUNT (row stride) while the arbiter request mask uses CL_M_COUNT (column stride). This pair aligns the row-major int_axis_tvalid layout with the arbiter's per-destination request encoding, eliminating the cross-talk that stalls grants and suppresses m_axis_tvalid at cycles 4-5."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_1c6d751771aa",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 311,
      "mechanism": "The arbiter grant_encoded index feeds the mux for s_axis_tvalid_mux, but the slice stride is written as M_COUNT instead of CL_S_COUNT. With M_COUNT=1 this collapses the mux to always select source 0, ignoring the round-robin grant and blocking valid propagation. Correcting the stride to CL_S_COUNT lets the granted source drive m_axis_tvalid."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_d4cc02256aa6",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 301,
      "mechanism": "The arbiter acknowledge mask shift uses S_COUNT as the width parameter while the index is only CL_S_COUNT bits. The overshoot creates an incorrect acknowledge mask, causing the BLOCK=\"ACKNOWLEDGE\" state to hold grants too long/short and desynchronize the valid pipeline. Replacing S_COUNT with CL_M_COUNT restores the proper mask width for the destination-side matrix."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_c1a2f86ca667",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 223,
      "mechanism": "Reset only clears select_valid_reg but leaves select_reg uninitialized. A stale select_reg from power-up combines with the first valid input to compute a wrong select_next, misrouting the initial transaction and delaying m_axis_tvalid. Explicitly resetting select_reg to 0 removes the stale pointer."
    }
  ]
  , "explanation": "The trace shows m_axis_tvalid stuck at 0 for cycles 4-5 when it should be 1, indicating the first packet from the source side is not reaching the output register. Root cause analysis of the original RTL identifies three intertwined defects: (1) missing reset of per-source drop_reg/select_reg leaves the routing state machine in an X state during early cycles; (2) matrix indexing uses literal parameter values (M_COUNT, S_COUNT) instead of their $clog2 widths, collapsing row/column addressing in the int_axis_tvalid and arbiter request/grant vectors; (3) the destination-range comparator runs during reset, injecting a spurious select_valid pulse. The top-ranked patch (09b2f4ca228b) directly clears the uninitialized routing registers so the first transaction is processed cleanly. The next patches correct the two matrix strides and the reset-time comparator gate, which together restore the end-to-end valid path. Lower-ranked patches address secondary stride mismatches in the arbiter mux and acknowledge logic that would surface once the primary path is fixed."
}
